<%# What stands behind the name at the head of a page: the picture of the thing
    the page is about, blown up until it is only light.

    Sixty-four pixels of it, and the browser stretches them across the whole
    header. That much of it was always true; what was not is what stood here
    saying that a picture stretched that far IS a blur. It is not. Stretching
    interpolates, and interpolating leaves the sleeve's edges exactly where they
    were, only wider — the lettering stayed legible and the JPEG's own grid came
    out as soft squares a hand's width across.

    So the edges come out before it is sent, in ffmpeg, once, on a picture already
    down to sixty-four pixels. Five hundred bytes, and nothing is asked of the
    phone at all. The stylesheet says what the mask and the scrim are for.

    Not the same file as the sharp sleeve in front of it, and that is the point:
    the sharp one is a picture, this one is a light. %>
<% if picture %>
  <div aria-hidden="true" class="hero-wash absolute inset-0 overflow-hidden rounded-t-2xl">
    <img src="<%= picture %>" alt="" loading="eager" decoding="async">
  </div>
<% else %>
  <%# Nothing to blow up: a record nobody scanned, an artist nobody photographed.
      The header keeps the quiet gradient it always had rather than inventing a
      colour for somebody who never gave us one. %>
  <div aria-hidden="true"
       class="absolute inset-0 rounded-t-2xl bg-gradient-to-b from-neutral-700/50 via-neutral-900/30 to-transparent"></div>
<% end %>
